Faecal Incontinence
Geriatric & Frailty
Involuntary passage of faeces, Sudden compelling rectal urgency, Passive seepage without awareness, Reduced anal squeeze pressure

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-018
Reason for Visit: Severe social distress, acute perianal pain, and continuous involuntary bowel leakage causing a hygiene crisis.
Location: Aged Care Facility
Present at Visit: Patient and nursing staff
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a distressing presentation of continuous, uncontrollable liquid stool seepage in a frail patient, resulting in severe perianal skin excoriation and acute pain. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to differentiate between an acute infectious diarrhoeal illness and severe faecal impaction with overflow, allowing for immediate targeted intervention and pain relief prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I am constantly dirtying myself without even feeling it happen, and my skin is burning so badly I can't sit down."
History of Present Illness: Over the past three days, the patient reports the involuntary passage of liquid faeces resulting in severe social embarrassment and distress. They describe frequent uncontrollable soiling of their underwear and the bed linen. Occasionally, they experience a sudden compelling urgency to open bowels but possess an inability to reach the toilet in time. However, the predominant symptom today is the passive seepage of liquid stool without any subjective awareness. A review of the facility charts reveals a preceding history of chronic severe constipation over the past two weeks. The patient is currently experiencing severe perianal excoriation and severe pruritus due to the persistent, highly acidic skin soiling. They deny fever, vomiting, or recent antibiotic use that might suggest an infectious cause.
Functional Status: Bedbound acutely due to perianal pain and fear of soiling; baseline mobilizes with a frame.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme loss of dignity and psychological distress; high risk of severe skin breakdown and secondary bacterial skin infection.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 142/85 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 71 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, lying on their side to avoid pressure on the buttocks, room has a strong faecal odor.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals a palpable, firm, non-tender mass in the left lower quadrant consistent with severe faecal loading. Bowel sounds are active. Inspection of the perianal area reveals severe, widespread perianal excoriation, erythema, and skin breakdown. Digital rectal examination (DRE) is exquisitely tender but reveals a massive, rock-hard faecal bolus in the rectal vault with liquid stool actively bypassing the impaction. DRE also demonstrates significantly reduced resting and squeeze pressure of the anal sphincter. (Identification of severe faecal loading on plain abdominal radiograph is an anticipated diagnostic tetrad finding, though clinically evident here).
Environmental Observations: Multiple soiled incontinence pads are bagged in the room; patient's dignity is severely compromised.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has been receiving daily loperamide (anti-diarrhoeal) from staff over the past 48 hours for the "diarrhoea," severely exacerbating the underlying impaction.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Faecal Impaction resulting in Overflow Faecal Incontinence - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Infectious Gastroenteritis (e.g., C. difficile) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of fever, abdominal cramping, or systemic illness, and the definitive presence of a massive hard faecal bolus on DRE.

Primary Sphincter Incompetence - Ruled out/unlikely due to: While sphincter tone is reduced, the primary driver of the acute liquid leakage is the mechanical overflow from the profound rectal impaction, rather than pure sphincter failure alone.

Inflammatory Bowel Disease Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of gross blood, mucus, or chronic crampy abdominal pain, with the clinical picture dominated by mechanical obstruction.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Skin integrity and psychological risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with distressing, continuous liquid faecal incontinence and severe perianal skin breakdown. The critical clinical error made prior to the visit was treating the overflow seepage as primary diarrhoea. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have resulted in continued administration of anti-motility agents, worsening the profound impaction, causing further agonizing skin excoriation, and risking bowel perforation. This urgent after-hours face-to-face assessment allowed for the crucial digital rectal examination, identifying the true mechanical cause (impaction), immediately ceasing harmful medications, and initiating the appropriate disimpaction protocol to resolve the incontinence and relieve the severe pain.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediately ceased all loperamide. Administered a micro-enema (Microlax) to begin softening the distal impaction. Prescribed a robust regimen of oral macrogol (Movicol) up to 8 sachets daily for rapid disimpaction. Prescribed a thick zinc oxide barrier cream (e.g., Sudocrem or specialized stoma paste) to be applied thickly after every pad change to protect the excoriated skin.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a structured hydration and skin protection routine for the nursing staff. Step 1: Staff offer the patient 200mL of water or juice every two hours while awake, and reapply the thick barrier cream at every single pad check, regardless of soiling. Step 2: Upon successful completion of a shift with zero new skin breakdown and adequate hydration, the staff visually update a 'comfort goal' chart in the patient's room. Cause-and-effect: Structuring the hydration to ensure the oral laxatives work, paired with religious application of barrier cream, directly heals the skin and resolves the impaction, rapidly restoring the patient's dignity and comfort.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Instructed facility staff to implement strict bowel and fluid charts.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommended a pressure-relieving cushion for the armchair once the patient is able to sit up.
Follow-up: Routine GP review required within 48-72 hours to ensure complete disimpaction and transition to a maintenance laxative regimen.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department if the patient develops severe, sudden abdominal pain, a rigid abdomen, high fever, or if they begin vomiting faeculent (stool-like) material.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The leakage is paradoxically caused by severe constipation; the treatment requires causing more bowel movements initially to clear the blockage.
Clinical Course: The oral medications will cause significant, soft bowel movements over the next 48 hours to clear the hard blockage. Once the blockage is gone, the constant leakage will stop, and your skin will heal rapidly.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

(Directed to nursing staff): Transfer the patient to the Emergency Department immediately if their abdomen becomes rigid and exquisitely painful, or if they begin vomiting. A GP must review the bowel chart within three days.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You do not have diarrhoea. You have a large, hard blockage of stool, and the liquid you are experiencing is simply seeping around that blockage. The anti-diarrhoea medication made the blockage much worse. We are giving you medications to flush the blockage out, and a thick cream to stop the liquid from burning your skin.
Actionable Steps: Stop taking any anti-diarrhoea medication. You must drink large amounts of water for the new clearing powders to work. Allow the nurses to apply the thick barrier cream constantly to heal your skin.

